# Which operations hand back a NEW reference (design.md §4.10; panels
# 021, 022). Port of crates/heroes/src/owning.rs.
#
# The ownership pass decides WHERE an incref and a release go; this
# decides WHAT needs one at all, and it is worth its own module because
# it is the question the pass gets wrong silently. Every other mistake
# in the ownership pass is a crash or a sanitiser report; a wrong
# answer here is a LEAK, which nothing in an ordinary build reports —
# it took the leak counter saying `3 heap blocks still live at exit` to
# find the first one.
#
# Exhaustive matches rather than allow-lists on purpose: a new IR op
# has to be classified here, loudly.

use ir

# Whether this operation hands back a new reference. Everything else
# either borrows (a load, a field read) or produces something
# uncounted. A call is owning because a Heroes function increfs what it
# returns; a builtin because the runtime's constructors return +1. A
# str literal is NOT: a literal is a static block whose refcount is
# negative, so nothing owns it.
#
# `+` on a str ALLOCATES, and forgetting it was this pass's first
# defect: three live blocks at exit, all tracing to one missing row.
# The concatenation is a binary, which reads like arithmetic and is a
# constructor — only the result type tells them apart, and this
# function is called only when the result is refcounted, so binary here
# can be nothing else.
#
# `m[k]` allocates, and it is the only one of its four lookalikes that
# does: a field read, an element read and a payload read hand back a
# borrowed view, but a map lookup BUILDS a V? around the value, and
# building it copies the value through its descriptor, which increfs.
# Found by the leak counter, and only with a heap-allocated value — a
# str literal's negative refcount made the same missing decref
# invisible.
function allocates(op: ir.OpKind) -> bool
    return match op
        .call | .construct | .cast | .binary => true
        .map_get => true
        .field | .index | .payload => false
        .lit | .load | .store | .unary | .len_of | .tag_of => false
        .func_ref | .copy_out | .abort_op | .incref | .decref => false
        .hole | .missing => false

# Whether a constructor makes its result a SECOND OWNER of every
# counted argument (rule 6). A record, a variant case and both sides of
# a T? all hold their argument by value, so the new value is a second
# owner. ok(x) was FIRST written as a wrap that consumes its argument,
# on panel 021's note — but rule 5 came later and made every value
# reaching a constructor borrowed, so consuming one is a release the
# program never made; the note was older than the rule. A container
# literal is built by push, whose copy already takes the array's own
# reference per element — listed one shape at a time, so that landing a
# container is a decision here.
function retains_fields(shape: ir.Shape) -> bool
    return match shape
        .record_shape | .case_shape => true
        .ok_shape | .err_shape | .fail_shape => true
        .array_shape | .map_shape => false

## Tests

test "the four constructors own, the three lookalike reads borrow"
    assert allocates(.call(callee: .builtin_fn(at: 0), arguments: ir.Run(start: 0, length: 0), variadic: false))
    assert allocates(.map_get(map: 1, key: 2))
    assert allocates(.binary(op: .add, left: 1, right: 2))
    assert !allocates(.field(base: 1, at: 0))
    assert !allocates(.index(base: 1, at: 2))
    assert !allocates(.payload(base: 1, at: 0))
    assert !allocates(.lit(value: .str_const(at: 0)))

test "a record is a second owner, a container literal is not"
    assert retains_fields(.record_shape(decl: 0))
    assert retains_fields(.ok_shape)
    assert !retains_fields(.array_shape)
    assert !retains_fields(.map_shape)
